{
  "summary": "This experiment isolated the effect of representation quality by replacing the linear PCA-2000 encoder with a learned scvi_n200 variational autoencoder in the otherwise identical RNA-only, single-profile configuration. The validation accuracy improved from ~48.5% (F1 ~46.4%) to ~52.0% (F1 ~49.8%), a modest ~3.5-point gain that indicates a learned nonlinear encoder captures some additional structure beyond linear PCA, yet the relatively small improvement also suggests the single-profile RNA signal may be partially data-limited rather than purely representation-limited. It remains uncertain whether this encoder advantage would compound in multiprofile aggregation or multimodal fusion settings, and whether the ~52% level is near the practical ceiling for RNA-only single-profile models or whether further architectural changes could yield substantially larger gains."
}

